QB.add(11,[{"n":1,"q":"You are walking in a park when you notice a person suddenly collapse and become unresponsive. You quickly assess the situation and suspect cardiac arrest. According to Basic Cardiac Life Support (BCLS) guidelines, what is the first appropriate action you should take?","o":[{"t":"Start chest compressions immediately"},{"t":"Check the scene for safety before approaching the victim"},{"t":"Call for help and get an AED before checking for responsiveness"},{"t":"Provide rescue breaths before checking for a pulse"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"According to Basic Cardiac Life Support (BCLS) guidelines, the first step is always to ensure the scene is safe . This is crucial to protect both the rescuer and the victim. If the area is unsafe (e.g., traffic, electrical hazards), it could lead to further harm."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Steps of Basic Cardiac Life Support (BCLS) outside the hospital:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ensure scene safety: before starting any BLS, ensure the environment is safe. Remove the victim from any immediate danger, such as standing water or traffic."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tap on the shoulder and speak loudly in a language the victim could understand to elicit a response from the victim."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Call for help , inform the emergency medical system, and get emergency equipment. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Check for the carotid pulse for 5–10 seconds and the breathing effort simultaneously by looking for chest rise. (vs Option D) Normal breathing with a definite carotid pulse : the victim needs to be assessed every 2 min or earlier to determine any change in condition. Abnormal or no breathing with a definite carotid pulse : the victim is in respiratory arrest and should be provided normal tidal volume breaths every 5 s or 12 breaths every minute using mouth-to-mouth, mouth-to-mask, or bag-mask device ventilation. Abnormal or no breathing without a definite carotid pulse : the victim is in cardiopulmonary arrest and requires high-quality CPR including cycles of compression and breaths at the earliest. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal breathing with a definite carotid pulse : the victim needs to be assessed every 2 min or earlier to determine any change in condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abnormal or no breathing with a definite carotid pulse : the victim is in respiratory arrest and should be provided normal tidal volume breaths every 5 s or 12 breaths every minute using mouth-to-mouth, mouth-to-mask, or bag-mask device ventilation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abnormal or no breathing without a definite carotid pulse : the victim is in cardiopulmonary arrest and requires high-quality CPR including cycles of compression and breaths at the earliest. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal breathing with a definite carotid pulse : the victim needs to be assessed every 2 min or earlier to determine any change in condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abnormal or no breathing with a definite carotid pulse : the victim is in respiratory arrest and should be provided normal tidal volume breaths every 5 s or 12 breaths every minute using mouth-to-mouth, mouth-to-mask, or bag-mask device ventilation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abnormal or no breathing without a definite carotid pulse : the victim is in cardiopulmonary arrest and requires high-quality CPR including cycles of compression and breaths at the earliest. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A cycle of CPR is 30 chest compressions: 2 breaths with the speed of chest compressions at 120/min and compression depth at 5–6 cm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Once the emergency equipment is available, like an AED or defibrillator, they are used for further resuscitation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiac Life Support (BCLS) outside the hospital\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8756\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"683\" Automated External Defibrillator:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Turn On the AED."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follow audio instructions: Listen to the voice prompts provided by the AED."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Attach pads: Place the AED pads on the victim’s chest while continuing chest compressions, one in the right infraclavicular region and 2nd below the left nipple into the axilla."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Analyze Heart Rhythm: allow the AED to assess the heart rhythm and ensure no one is touching the victim during this analysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deliver Shock: if the AED indicates, administer the electric shock as directed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Resume CPR: immediately continue CPR, starting with chest compressions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In-hospital resuscitation:"},{"k":"img","v":4853},{"k":"img","v":4856},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Learning Objective:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Basic Life Support (BLS) and Advanced Cardiovascular Life Support (ACLS)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Basic Life Support (BLS) Advanced Cardiovascular Life"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Support (ACLS)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Goal Maintain brain perfusion by Maintain cardiovascular function"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"supporting circulation and breathing. with advanced interventions,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"including airway management, rhythm correction, and medication administration."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Primary Users First responders (e.g., EMTs, Healthcare professionals (e.g.,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"nurses, rescuers with CPR physicians, paramedics, nurses with certification). advanced training)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Airway Manage Basic methods: mouth-to-mask, Advanced airway techniques: ment bag-mask ventilation. endotracheal intubation, supraglottic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"airway devices."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Breathing Rescue breaths or bag-mask Continuous ventilation with Support ventilation. advanced airway in place (1 breath"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"every 6 seconds)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Use of Defibrilla AED (Automated External A defibrillator is used, but the tion Defibrillator) automatically detects healthcare provider makes the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"rhythm and advises shock if needed. decision to shock based on rhythm"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"analysis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Basic Life Support (BLS) Advanced Cardiovascular Life"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Support (ACLS)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Differential Focuses on performing CPR and Includes diagnosing and treating Diagnosis of defibrillation without diagnosing reversible causes of pulselessness Pulselessness underlying causes. like hypovolemia, tension"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, etc."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medication Use No medications were administered. Administers medications (e.g.,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"epinephrine, amiodarone, atropine)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chest Compres High-quality chest compressions Same chest compression sions (30:2 ratio for adults without techniques, with rhythm-guided"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"advanced airway). interruptions for defibrillation or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"other interventions."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Focus on Not explicitly emphasized. Actively diagnoses and treats Reversible reversible causes (e.g., 5 H’s and Causes T’s)"}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 55-year-old patient with sepsis is admitted to the ICU. During the hospital stay, the patient suddenly becomes unresponsive, pulseless, and apneic. An immediate ECG is performed, revealing chaotic, irregular waveforms without identifiable QRS complexes or P waves. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Asystole"},{"t":"Pulseless Electrical Activity"},{"t":"Ventricular Tachycardia"},{"t":"Ventricular Fibrillation"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The mentioned features (unresponsiveness, no pulse, and no effective breathing) combined with the ECG showing chaotic, irregular waveforms without distinct QRS complexes or P waves are diagnostic of ventricular fibrillation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immediate unsynchronized defibrillation is the primary treatment to restore a normal heart rhythm."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulseless Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In cardiac arrest, the absence of a palpable pulse indicates a lack of effective circulation. Cardiac arrest rhythms are categorized into two main types: shockable and non-shockable."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Shockable & Non-Shockable Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Shockable Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular Fibrillation: is a life-threatening arrhythmia characterized by chaotic, irregular waveforms on the ECG with no identifiable QRS complexes or P waves. It results in ineffective quivering of the ventricles, leading to a complete loss of cardiac output and immediate loss of consciousness. Ventricular Fibrillation\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8763\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"189\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/UvsEFj8FghHUawIAdirz1744302492.png\" width=\"795\" /> Shockable Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular Tachycardia (Option C): It is characterized by a rapid heart rate originating from the ventricles, typically defined as three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a rate exceeding 100 beats per minute. The ECG will show wide QRS complexes (‡0.12 seconds) that appear similar and occur regularly. In some cases, VT can still have a pulse. Ventricular Tachycardia\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8765\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"300\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/PP7Q3Ua61DQtXxeGIbow1744302618.png\" width=\"582\" />"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These are classified as shockable rhythms, meaning they can be treated with defibrillation. Survival rates for shockable rhythms are generally higher, especially with early defibrillation and high-quality CPR."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Survival rates are around 20-40% when immediate treatment is provided."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Shockable Conditions"},{"k":"img","v":4842},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Asystole (Option A): It is a state of no electrical activity in the heart, resulting in a flat-line ECG with no discernible waves (no P waves, QRS complexes, or T waves). It indicates a complete absence of cardiac activity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulseless Electrical Activity (Option B): It is a condition where the ECG shows organized electrical activity (which may appear as normal sinus rhythm or other patterns), but the heart fails to contract effectively, resulting in no palpable pulse. Pulseless Electrical Activity\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8769\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"153\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/UKNfnBH9k7h4zBglbM111744302756.png\" width=\"530\" />"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These rhythms are non-shockable, and defibrillation is not indicated. Treatment focuses on high-quality CPR and identifying and treating reversible causes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Survival rates are much lower than for shockable rhythms, with survival often below 10%."}],"qi":[4836]},{"n":3,"q":"A 62-year-old man is brought after collapsing at home. Upon arrival, he is found to be in cardiac arrest. The medical team begins resuscitation efforts and considers potential reversible causes. Which of the following is not considered one of the reversible causes of cardiac arrest?","o":[{"t":"Hypovolemia"},{"t":"Tension pneumothorax"},{"t":"Hypertrophied ventricle"},{"t":"Hypoxia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A hypertrophied ventricle refers to a thickened heart muscle, typically due to chronic conditions like hypertension or valvular disease ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While it can contribute to heart failure or arrhythmias, it is not considered an immediate reversible cause of cardiac arrest in the same way that factors like hypovolemia, tension pneumothorax, or hypoxia are."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The treatment of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, for example, often involves long-term management with medications, lifestyle changes, or surgical interventions, rather than an acute, life-saving intervention during cardiac arrest."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reversible causes of cardiac arrest, often referred to as the \"Hs and Ts,\" represent conditions that, if identified and corrected promptly, can potentially reverse a cardiac arrest and restore normal circulation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"These causes are divided into two main groups:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic disturbances (the \"Hs\")"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mechanical causes (the \"Ts\")"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reversible Causes of Cardiac Arrest: H's and T's (Options A, B & D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"H's T's"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypovolemia Tension pneumothorax"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypoxia Tamponade (cardiac)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hydrogen ion (acidosis) Toxins"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypo-/Hyperkalemia Thrombosis (coronary MI"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypothermia Thrombosis (pulmonary embolism)"}]},{"n":4,"q":"Assertion: Cardioversion is the preferred treatment for symptomatic supraventricular tachycardia (SVT). Reason: Synchronized direct current (DC) shock is administered at the peak of the R wave to effectively restore normal sinus rhythm while minimizing the risk of ventricular fibrillation.","o":[{"t":"Both assertion and reason are true, and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion."},{"t":"Both assertion and reason are true, but the reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion."},{"t":"The assertion is true, but the reason is false."},{"t":"The assertion is false, but the reason is true."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Assertion: Cardioversion is preferred for symptomatic supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) because it is the most effective and rapid method of restoring normal sinus rhythm, particularly in hemodynamically unstable patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reason: Synchronized DC shock is delivered at the peak of the R wave to avoid the vulnerable period of the cardiac cycle (the T wave). Delivering a shock during the T wave can precipitate dangerous arrhythmias, such as ventricular fibrillation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This careful timing is essential to minimize the risk associated with the R and T phenomenon , which refers to the potential for inducing arrhythmias when a shock is applied during these critical phases of the cardiac cycle."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Cardioversion Defibrillation"},{"k":"img","v":4866},{"k":"img","v":4871},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definitio n"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"A synchronized electrical shock to reset the abnormal heart rhythm."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"An unsynchronized shock to correct life-threatening arrhythmias."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mechan Synchronized with the R wave in the Delivered irrespective of the R wave ism ECG (delivered at the peak of the R (unsynchronized)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"wave)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Physiol Stops abnormal contractions to Halts all electrical activity in the ogical allow the heart to reset, followed by heart, hoping normal rhythm restarts Effect the SA node resuming normal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"activity"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Indicatio Performed in arrhythmias where the Performed in life-threatening ns heart is still contracting but arrhythmias where the heart is not"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"irregularly, such as: pumping blood effectively, such as: Paroxysmal Supraventricular Ventricular Fibrillation (VF) Tachycardia (PSVT) Pulseless Ventricular Tachycardia Atrial Fibrillation (AF) (VT) Atrial Flutter"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Energy Usually lower energy levels (e.g., Usually higher energy levels (e.g., Level 50-100 joules for AF) 150-360 joules for VF)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Perform Elective procedure, typically not in Emergencies (like during a cardiac ed in emergencies. arrest)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complic Less risk of inducing dangerous Higher risk of inducing other ations arrhythmias due to synchronization. arrhythmias (e.g., R-on-T"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"phenomenon) if the shock is delivered improperly."}]},{"n":5,"q":"In an ICU, during cardiac resuscitation, a flat line is observed on the ECG. The attending physician instructs the team to administer adrenaline and continue CPR. What is the primary objective of administering adrenaline in this situation?","o":[{"t":"To immediately restore normal sinus rhythm."},{"t":"To convert asystole into ventricular fibrillation."},{"t":"To increase myocardial oxygen demand."},{"t":"To decrease preload on the heart."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administering adrenaline in the context of asystole aims to increase the likelihood of developing a shockable rhythm such as VF, which is necessary for effective defibrillation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Resuscitation Steps for Asystole"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Identify Non-Shockable Rhythm (Asystole) Activate Emergency Response: Call for advanced life support. fl Initiate CPR Start High-Quality CPR: Perform chest compressions at a rate of 100-120/min, with a depth of 5-6 cm. Continue CPR for 2 minutes. fl Establish intravenous or intraosseous lines for medication administration. fl Administer Epinephrine Epinephrine 1 mg (1:10,000): Administer IV/IO after the first 2 minutes of CPR. fl Identify Rhythm Non-Shockable Rhythm: Continue CPR and administer epinephrine every 3-5 minutes. Goal: To increase the likelihood of developing a shockable rhythm, such as ventricular fibrillation (VF), which is necessary for effective defibrillation. Shockable Rhythm: Administer a Non-Synchronized DC Shock to restore normal rhythm. fl Advanced Airway Management Airway: Secure the airway with endotracheal intubation if necessary. Capnography: Monitor end-tidal CO2 levels. Ventilation: Provide 1 breath every 6 seconds post-intubation for adequate oxygenation. fl Continue CPR Resume chest compressions for 2 minutes. fl Identify and Treat Reversible Causes Primary Focus: Address reversible causes, especially hypothermia. fl Repeat CPR and Epinephrine Cycle: Continue repeating CPR and epinephrine administration every 3-5 minutes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To immediately restore normal sinus rhythm (Option A): This is incorrect . Adrenaline (epinephrine) does not directly restore sinus rhythm, especially in asystole. It enhances perfusion pressure during CPR, increasing the chances of return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC), but it does not directly induce organized rhythm like a defibrillator would in shockable rhythms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To increase myocardial oxygen demand (Option C): This is incorrect . While adrenaline does increase oxygen demand, this is not a therapeutic goal during cardiac arrest. In fact, increasing oxygen demand without perfusion is detrimental. The primary aim is to increase coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure during resuscitation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To decrease preload on the heart (Option D): This is incorrect . Adrenaline actually increases venous return (preload) by causing peripheral vasoconstriction. Decreasing preload is not desirable during CPR, where maximizing perfusion to vital organs is critical."}],"qi":[4842]},{"n":6,"q":"A 65-year-old male in the ICU experiences cardiac arrest, during which ventricular fibrillation is observed. Continuous cardiopulmonary resuscitation is in progress, and a defibrillator has been used multiple times, but the patient remains unresponsive. What is the next appropriate step in the management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Administer Epinephrine"},{"t":"Perform Advanced Airway Management"},{"t":"Continue CPR with Compressions"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Amiodarone is an antiarrhythmic medication used in cases of persistent ventricular fibrillation (VF) after multiple defibrillation attempts. It works by prolonging the action potential and refractory period in myocardial tissue."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Resuscitation Steps for Shockable & Non-Shockable Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Shockable Conditions Non-Shockable Conditions"},{"k":"img","v":4842},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular Fibrillation Ventricular Fibrillation\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8763\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"125\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/U vsEFj8FghHUawIAdirz1744302492.png\" width=\"525\" /> Asystole"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular Tachycardia Pulseless Electrical Activity Ventricular Tachycardia\" data-author=\"\" Pulseless Electrical Activity\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8765\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-hash=\"8769\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"249\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"191\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"QtXxeGIbow1744302618.png\" width=\"482\" /> zBglbM111744302756.png\" width=\"662\" /> Shockable Conditions Non-Shockable Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immediate Recognition Immediate Recognition Identify Shockable Rhythm: Recognize VF or pulseless Identify Non-Shockable Rhythm: Recognize asystole or VT. pulseless electrical activity (PEA). Activate Emergency Response: Call for advanced life Activate Emergency Response: Call for advanced life support. support. fl fl Initiate CPR Initiate CPR Start High-Quality CPR: Perform chest compressions at Start High-Quality CPR: Perform chest compressions at a rate of 100-120/min, with a depth of 5-6 cm. Continue a rate of 100-120/min, with a depth of 5-6 cm. Continue CPR for 2 minutes. CPR for 2 minutes. fl fl Defibrillation Establish intravenous or intraosseous line for medication After 2 minutes of CPR, deliver a Non-Synchronized DC administration. Shock to restore normal rhythm. fl If no change, administer a second shock immediately. Administer Epinephrine fl Epinephrine 1 mg (1:10,000): Administer IV/IO after the Continue CPR first 2 minutes of CPR. Resume CPR for 2 Minutes fl Place an intravenous or intraosseous line for drug Identify Rhythm administration. Non-Shockable Rhythm: Continue CPR and administer fl epinephrine every 3-5 minutes. Administer Epinephrine Goal: To increase the likelihood of developing a Epinephrine 1 mg (1:10,000): Given IV/IO after 2 shockable rhythm, such as ventricular fibrillation (VF), minutes of CPR to enhance coronary perfusion pressure. which is necessary for effective defibrillation . fl Shockable Rhythm: Administer a Non-Synchronized DC Advanced Airway Management Shock to restore normal rhythm. Secure Airway: Perform endotracheal intubation if fl necessary. Advanced Airway Management Capnography: Monitor end-tidal CO2 levels. Airway: Secure the airway with endotracheal intubation Ventilation: Provide 1 breath every 6 seconds if necessary. post-intubation. Capnography: Monitor end-tidal CO2 levels. fl Ventilation: Provide 1 breath every 6 seconds Further Defibrillation post-intubation for adequate oxygenation. Third Shock: If VF or pulseless VT persists, deliver a fl third non-synchronized shock. Continue CPR Continue CPR for 2 More Minutes: Follow with Resume chest compressions for 2 minutes. additional CPR. fl fl Identify and Treat Reversible Causes Administer Antiarrhythmic Medication Primary Focus: Address reversible causes, especially Amiodarone 300 mg IV: Administer for shock-refractory hypothermia. VT to stabilize heart rhythm. fl fl Repeat CPR and Epinephrine Identify and Treat Reversible Causes Cycle: Continue repeating CPR and epinephrine"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"administration every 3-5 minutes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administer Epinephrine (Option B): Epinephrine is typically administered after the first or second shock, not as the immediate next step after multiple shocks have failed."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Perform Advanced Airway Management (Option C): Securing the airway is a critical component of resuscitation. It is not the next immediate step in managing persistent VF. The primary goal at this moment is to restore a viable cardiac rhythm through pharmacological intervention before addressing airway management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continue CPR with Compressions (Option D) : Continuing CPR is vital, but if the patient remains in VF despite multiple shocks, the introduction of an antiarrhythmic agent like amiodarone is necessary."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A patient is on mechanical ventilation. Which of the following statements is correct regarding the management of their ventilation parameters?","o":[{"t":"A tidal volume of 10 mL/kg lean body weight is recommended for ARDS patients."},{"t":"The respiratory rate should not be increased in cases of raised intracranial pressure."},{"t":"FiO2 was initially set at 1.0 and then reduced to maintain adequate oxygenation."},{"t":"PEEP typically ranges from 8 to 10 cm of H2O for optimal alveolar recruitment."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FIO2 (Fraction of Inspired Oxygen):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initially set at 1.0 (100% oxygen) and then reduced to the lowest level needed to maintain adequate oxygenation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Higher FiO2 increases the amount of oxygen delivered to the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If the FiO2 required >0.6, additional interventions should be considered to improve arterial oxygenation, especially in ARDS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In critically ill patients, FiO2 is used to assess lung gas exchange capacity through the PaO2/FiO2 (P/F) ratio, where PaO2 indicates the partial pressure of oxygen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tidal Volume: (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follow 12-12 Rule:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tidal volume: 12 mL/kg lean body weight."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory rate: 12 breaths per minute."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"COPD: Follow 10-10 Rule: Tidal volume: 10 mL/kg lean body weight. Respiratory rate: 10 breaths per minute. Recommended to prevent lung overinflation and hyperventilation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follow 10-10 Rule:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tidal volume: 10 mL/kg lean body weight."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory rate: 10 breaths per minute."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recommended to prevent lung overinflation and hyperventilation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ARDS: Lung-Protective Ventilation: Tidal volume: 6-8 mL/kg lean body weight. Recommended to prevent barotrauma and volume trauma. The respiratory rate may need to be increased to compensate for lower tidal volumes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tidal volume: 6-8 mL/kg lean body weight."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recommended to prevent barotrauma and volume trauma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The respiratory rate may need to be increased to compensate for lower tidal volumes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follow 10-10 Rule:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tidal volume: 10 mL/kg lean body weight."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory rate: 10 breaths per minute."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recommended to prevent lung overinflation and hyperventilation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tidal volume: 6-8 mL/kg lean body weight."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recommended to prevent barotrauma and volume trauma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The respiratory rate may need to be increased to compensate for lower tidal volumes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Respiratory Rate: (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Minute volume = Respiratory rate × Tidal volume Has an excellent correlation with metabolic demand."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Has an excellent correlation with metabolic demand."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Has an excellent correlation with metabolic demand."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conditions where the respiratory rate is increased: Raised intracranial pressure: Hyperventilation causes cerebral vasoconstriction-related reduction of cerebral blood volume (CBV), thereby decreasing intracranial pressure (ICP) and relaxing the brain. Diabetic ketoacidosis: In diabetic ketoacidosis, metabolic acidosis triggers hyperventilation, reducing CO2 to help prevent further decreases in pH and bicarbonate levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Raised intracranial pressure: Hyperventilation causes cerebral vasoconstriction-related reduction of cerebral blood volume (CBV), thereby decreasing intracranial pressure (ICP) and relaxing the brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic ketoacidosis: In diabetic ketoacidosis, metabolic acidosis triggers hyperventilation, reducing CO2 to help prevent further decreases in pH and bicarbonate levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Raised intracranial pressure: Hyperventilation causes cerebral vasoconstriction-related reduction of cerebral blood volume (CBV), thereby decreasing intracranial pressure (ICP) and relaxing the brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic ketoacidosis: In diabetic ketoacidosis, metabolic acidosis triggers hyperventilation, reducing CO2 to help prevent further decreases in pH and bicarbonate levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"PEEP (Positive End-Expiratory Pressure): (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Maintains lung volume during exhalation, preventing alveolar collapse and improving oxygenation by reducing ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily used to maximize alveolar recruitment and prevent overdistension during tidal ventilation, rather than solely to improve gas exchange."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physiologic PEEP typically ranges from 3 to 5 cm of H2O."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Disadvantages: Excessive PEEP fi Increases intrathoracic pressure fi Compressing the right atrium fi Reduced venous return fi Hypotension, and elevating plateau pressure, which raises the risk of barotrauma (e.g., pneumothorax)."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 58-year-old male patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is admitted to the hospital and placed on mechanical ventilation. His arterial blood gas analysis shows the following values: PaO2: 86 mmHg, PaCO2: 50 mmHg and pH: 7.32. Which of the following management strategies should be prioritized for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Decrease the tidal volume to improve ventilation."},{"t":"Increase the FiO2 immediately to avoid oxygen toxicity."},{"t":"Increase PEEP to improve oxygenation and maintain lung recruitment."},{"t":"Increase the respiratory rate to enhance CO2 removal and improve pH."}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Elevated PaCO2 (normal: 35-45 mm Hg) and low pH (normal: 7.35-7.45) points towards diagnosis of Respiratory acidosis. Increasing the respiratory rate will help remove CO2, thereby lowering the elevated PaCO2 and improving the patient's pH, which is crucial for maintaining acid-base balance."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Patient A Patient B Patient C"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Par pH: 7.4 (Normal) pH: 7.2 (Acidosis) pH: 7.44 (Alkalosis) am pO2: 60 (Deficient) pO2: 100 (Normal) pO2: 100 (Normal) eter pCO2: 40 (Normal) pCO2: 60 (Increased) pCO2: 30 (Decreased) s"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dia Hypoxemia Respiratory Acidosis Respiratory Alkalosis gno sis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ma › FiO2 to improve › Respiratory rate to fl Respiratory rate to allow nag oxygenation (Option B) remove CO2, thus CO2 accumulation in em But prolonged high FiO2 lowering PaCO2. (Option alveoli, thereby increasing ent can lead to oxygen toxicity D) PaCO2"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Therefore maintain target › Minute volume fl Tidal volume (Vt) can › PaO2 with the lowest › Tidal volume (Vt) also PaCO2 levels, but with FiO2 required. enhances CO2 removal. caution to ensure › PEEP to keep alveoli adequate ventilation. open. (Option C) (Option A) Optimal PEEP enhances gas exchange and reduces the risk of atelectasis."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A post-surgery patient is placed on mechanical ventilation, and the medical team is deciding whether to continue using Assist-Control Ventilation (ACV) or switch to Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation (SIMV) to support the weaning process. Which of the following statements about ACV and SIMV is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"ACV provides both patient-triggered and time-triggered breaths for full support."},{"t":"SIMV allows spontaneous breaths between mandatory breaths, aiding in weaning."},{"t":"ACV reduces the work of breathing and helps manage respiratory acidosis/alkalosis."},{"t":"SIMV cannot adjust automatically to maintain minute ventilation"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This statement is incorrect because many modern SIMV modes are designed to adapt and maintain a target minute ventilation . While traditional SIMV provides a set number of mandatory breaths and allow for spontaneous breaths between them, newer ventilators often include adaptive features."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mechanica Assist-Control Ventilation (ACV) Synchronized Intermittent l Ventilatio Mandatory Ventilation (SIMV) n Mode"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Features The mechanical ventilator is set at: Volume control ventilation mode."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Tidal volume (VT): 500 ml Delivers a set number of mandatory Respiratory Rate: 12/minute breaths with a preset volume while FiO2: 40% allowing spontaneous breaths when The most commonly used mode of airway pressure drops below invasive mechanical ventilation in end-expiratory pressure. intensive care units. Key feature is that it provides a supported breath for both patient-triggered or time-triggered breaths."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Advantage Increases patient comfort by SIMV is used to help wean patients s allowing them to trigger breaths as from the ventilator."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"needed. Adjusts automatically to maintain Enables easy management of CO2 minute ventilation. levels to correct respiratory Allows normal alveolar ventilation, acidosis/alkalosis. avoiding acute respiratory alkalosis Reduces the patient's work of by preserving the patient's breathing. ventilatory drive."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disadvant Volume-cycled ventilation can lead There is a potential for asynchrony ages to barotrauma, especially in stiff between patient and ventilator,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"lungs; monitoring plateau pressures where a machine-driven inspiration is crucial. may overlap with the patient's Tachypnea or insufficient exhalation spontaneous exhalation. time can cause breath stacking and This can increase the work of auto-PEEP, increasing intrathoracic breathing, potentially leading to pressure and leading to hypoventilation or pneumothorax. hypotension; disconnecting the ventilator briefly allows full exhalation. Hyperventilation may occur, causing respiratory alkalosis; adequate sedation can prevent this."},{"k":"img","v":4880},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Image Ventilation \" data-author=\"\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-hash=\"8785\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"otes/NGxNyAxn8CL2otx42vF417443 03656.png\" />"}]},{"n":10,"q":"A patient on mechanical ventilation is being monitored using ventilator waveforms. Based on the waveform interpretations, which of the following statements is correct?","o":[{"t":"The upslope of the volume vs. time scalar represents the expiratory volume."},{"t":"A negative value on the flow vs. time scalar represents expiratory flow."},{"t":"PEEP is indicated by the peak inspiratory pressure on the pressure vs. time scalar."},{"t":"The middle steep part of the pressure-volume curve indicates low lung compliance."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventilator waveforms:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Graphical representations of breath delivery to a patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Include 3 scalars and 2 Loops: Flow vs. time, volume vs. time, pressure vs. time, pressure-volume loop and flow-volume loop"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Volume vs. Time Scalar Flow vs. Time Scalar Pressure vs. Time Scalar"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Graphically represents the amount Represents gas flow between Displays pressure in the airway as a of gas delivered into the lungs over the patient and the ventilator. function of time, which can be fixed time by the ventilator. Inspiratory Flow: Positive or variable depending on the mode Upslope: Represents the inspiratory value on the graph. of ventilation. volume. (Option A) Expiratory Flow: Negative Baseline Pressure: Indicates PEEP Downslope: Represents the value on the graph. (Option B) (Positive End-Expiratory Pressure). expiratory volume. The area under the curve: (Option C) Use: Helps to identify air leaks, air Indicates the volume moved Peak Inspiratory Pressure (PIP): trapping, and patient’s spontaneous during breathing phases. Maximum pressure at the end of the breathing efforts and is useful for Use: To identify the mode of curve. evaluating changes in tidal volume ventilation. It provides Plateau Pressure (Pplat): with adjustments in ventilator information about the patient's Represents airway pressure under settings. exhalation. static conditions (no airflow),"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"obtained during an inspiratory hold. Use: Provides valuable information about airway compliance over time."},{"k":"img","v":4883},{"k":"img","v":4891},{"k":"img","v":4898},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pressure-Volume Curve Flow-Volume Curve"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Shows pressure (cm water) along the x-axis It shows volume (mL) on the x-axis and flow rate (L/min) and volume (mL) along the y-axis. on the y-axis. Inspiratory curve forms a sigmoidal shape: Inspiratory Limb : Flow reaches zero at the end of Initial Flat Part: Represents air movement into inspiration. collapsed airways with low compliance. Expiratory Limb : Curve completes when both volume Middle Steep Part: Indicates lung recruitment. and flow return to zero, indicating the end of expiration. (Option D) Obstructive patterns : Show a “scooped out” Flattening at End: Represents the end of appearance on the expiratory limb, with concavity inspiration. indicating lower flow at a given volume. Hysteresis : Failure of the lung tissue to act the same with inspiration and expiration. Expiratory curve resembles a football shape. Flatter Slope : Indicates decreased lung compliance. Steeper Slope : Indicates increased lung compliance."},{"k":"img","v":4901},{"k":"img","v":4904}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 65-year-old male with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is on mechanical ventilation. His healthcare team is evaluating the best ventilation mode to optimize his care. Which of the following statements regarding Pressure Control Ventilation (PCV) and Pressure Support Ventilation (PSV) is correct, particularly if the patient’s respiratory effort is minimal?","o":[{"t":"PCV guarantees a specific tidal volume regardless of lung compliance."},{"t":"In PSV, tidal volume is guaranteed and does not depend on the patient's effort."},{"t":"PCV limits peak airway pressures, beneficial for patients at high risk of barotrauma."},{"t":"PSV is used for heavily sedated patients with no spontaneous breathing efforts."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In Pressure Control Ventilation (PCV) , the ventilator delivers breaths at a preset pressure, which helps prevent excessively high airway pressures. This is especially useful in patients with ARDS , who have stiff (non-compliant) lungs and are at increased risk of barotrauma (lung injury due to high pressures). While tidal volume may vary, keeping pressure within safe limits helps protect the lungs."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pressure Control Ventilation (PCV) Pressure Support Ventilation (PSV)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mec Tidal volume in PCV varies PSV is entirely patient-triggered and hani depending on lung compliance. controlled. sm (Option A) Tidal volume is not guaranteed in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PSV and depends on lung compliance and the patient’s ability to sustain an inspiratory effort. (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pressure Control Ventilation (PCV) Pressure Support Ventilation (PSV)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Adva PCV is useful for limiting peak Patient effort is preserved and ntag airway and lung distending (plateau) controls tidal volume, inspiratory es pressures. flow and respiratory rate, allowing"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It is often used in conditions where for ventilator synchrony. high pressures could cause harm, Commonly used in patients who are such as ARDS or after thoracic less sedated and able to participate surgery. (Option C) in breathing, often during weaning Limit barotrauma if the patient's from mechanical ventilation or when respiratory effort is minimal. the ventilator is used mainly for Inspiratory flow rate can vary with airway support. (Option D) patient effort (improved comfort/synchrony)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disa PCV does not assure a specific tidal Apnea and hypoventilation are dvan volume, so tidal volume and minute possible tage ventilation need to be monitored. Must monitor respiratory rate, tidal s Spontaneously breathing patients volume and minute ventilation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"on PCV can generate negative closely. pressure in the circuit, leading to increased inspiratory flow rates and tidal volumes, which raises transalveolar pressure and heightens the risk of volume or barotrauma."},{"k":"img","v":4907},{"k":"img","v":4909},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Imag es"}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 45-year-old male with congestive heart failure presents with acute shortness of breath, orthopnea, and bilateral pulmonary crackles. ABG reveals hypoxemia. Which of the following interventions will improve both oxygenation and reduce preload and afterload in this patient?","o":[{"t":"High-flow nasal oxygen"},{"t":"Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP)"},{"t":"Non-rebreather mask"},{"t":"Intubation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CPAP delivers continuous positive pressure , which helps maintain airway patency, improves oxygenation, and reduces both preload and afterload by increasing intrathoracic pressure . This makes it particularly beneficial for patients with pulmonary congestion and hypoxemia due toheart failure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continuous positive airway pressure ( CPAP)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"airway pressure (cpap) machine\" data-author=\"PruebasBMA\" data-hash=\"8802\" data-license=\"CC BY-SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuous_positive_airway_pressure#/media/File:CPAP.png\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"329\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/POVEhT07doVMwVEHZzFq174430501 5.png\" width=\"393\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CPAP delivers constant positive pressure to the airways throughout the respiratory cycle (inspiration and expiration). It helps maintain airway patency, improve oxygenation, reduce atelectasis, and enhance ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Indication"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA) : Maintains airway patency in patients with conditions like obesity, hypotonia, and adenotonsillar hypertrophy. Neonatal Respiratory Distress : Used in NICU for preterm infants with underdeveloped lungs and surfactant deficiency. Infant Respiratory Conditions : Treats hypoxia, bronchiolitis, pneumonia, and tracheomalacia by reducing the work of breathing. Hypoxic Respiratory Failure : Improves oxygenation and cardiac output in heart failure patients, promoting better V/Q matching. Pre-Intubation Support : Provides positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) to enhance oxygenation before intubation. Extubation Support : Helps extubated patients (e.g., those with OSA or heart failure) who still benefit from positive pressure. Heart Failure : Reduces the need for intubation in acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema by decreasing preload and afterload. (Option C) Chronic Pulmonary Diseases (COPD and Asthma) : BiPAP reduces the work of breathing, improves gas exchange, and prevents respiratory failure progression. Acute Bronchitis and Pneumonia : Improves oxygenation and reduces breathing effort, especially in patients with chronic lung disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Contraindications"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is not recommended for patients with poor respiratory drive, severe anxiety, unstable cardiorespiratory status, facial trauma, copious secretions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Equipment and Techniques"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CPAP machines consist of a motor, air filter, tubing, and mask interface (nasal, nasopharyngeal, or full-face mask). Special CPAP systems like bubble CPAP are used for neonates. Humidifiers and auto-titrating machines improve patient comfort and therapy effectiveness."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complications"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common side effects include nasal congestion, dry mouth, bloating, and mask discomfort. Compliance is a major issue, and patient monitoring is crucial to adjust settings and address discomfort."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-flow nasal oxygen (Option A) : Provides supplemental oxygen but does not reduce preload and afterload. It may relieve hypoxemia without addressing fluid overload."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"nasal oxygen\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8804\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"417\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/YbKDbk68y5pPCQ RN3yXx1744305099.png\" width=\"417\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-rebreather mask (Option C) : Delivers high concentrations of oxygen, improving oxygenation, but does not address hemodynamic issues or fluid overload in heart failure patients."},{"k":"img","v":4916},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intubation (Option D) : An invasive procedure that can provide mechanical ventilation and improve oxygenation but is typically reserved for severe respiratory failure and does not inherently reduce preload and afterload. It may complicate the patient's condition if done prematurely."},{"k":"img","v":4921}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 68-year-old male with a history of COPD presents with increasing dyspnea, productive cough, and confusion. Arterial blood gas (ABG) shows pH 7.30, pCOn 58 mmHg, and pOn 55 mmHg. He is hemodynamically stable. What is the next best step in management?","o":[{"t":"Immediate endotracheal intubation"},{"t":"Noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation (NIV)"},{"t":"Intravenous antibiotics only"},{"t":"High-flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient with COPD exacerbation shows moderate hypercarbia (pH 7.30, pCOn 58 mmHg) and respiratory acidosis , which is an indication of NIV . NIV, such as BiPAP, can reduce the work of breathing, improve gas exchange, and prevent the need for intubation. Since the patient is hemodynamically stable and able to protect his airway, NIV is the appropriate next step."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Noninvasive Positive-Pressure Ventilation (NIV)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NIV refers to positive-pressure ventilation delivered through a nasal or full-face mask, either at a continuous pressure (CPAP) or with varying inspiratory and expiratory pressures (BiPAP). Modes: Modern noninvasive ventilators can provide full support, including volume control ventilation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Indications"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute respiratory failure: This treatment is effective for conditions that respond quickly to treatment, reducing the need for prolonged mechanical ventilation. COPD exacerbations: NIV benefits moderate hypercarbia (pH 7.25-7.35), reducing the need for intubation and hospital stay. Severe respiratory acidosis (pH < 7.2) may require mechanical ventilation with intubation. (Option B) Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema: Acts as adjunctive therapy, improving gas exchange and respiratory mechanics alongside diuresis and vasodilators. Chronic respiratory failure: This is useful in restrictive lung diseases (e.g., severe scoliosis, respiratory muscle weakness) and chronic hypercapnia in COPD, reducing hospital admissions when used nocturnally."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Advantages"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reduces work of breathing Requires less sedation compared to Improves pulmonary compliance invasive ventilation Recruits collapsed (atelectatic) alveoli Shortens hospital stay"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lowers intubation rates without the risks of endotracheal intubation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Contraindications Noninvasive Positive-Pressure Ventilation (NIV)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inability to protect the airway (e.g., severe Facial trauma/surgery encephalopathy) Upper airway obstruction High aspiration risk (e.g., vomiting, upper GI bleeding) Significant hemodynamic instability Difficulty clearing respiratory secretions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immediate endotracheal intubation (Option A) : Reserved for severe respiratory acidosis (pH < 7.2) or failed NIV. This patient’s pH is 7.30, indicating moderate acidosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intravenous antibiotics only (Option C) : While antibiotics may be needed, they do not address acute respiratory failure or hypercarbia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy (Option D) : Helps with oxygenation but does not manage hypercapnia or improve ventilation as NIV does in COPD exacerbations."}]}]);
